{"reference":"Ezekiel 34","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":34,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Shepherds and the Flock","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter uses shepherd-and-flock language to condemn leaders who benefit themselves while neglecting those in their care. It then contrasts them with Jehovah, who says he will seek, gather, feed, and judge the flock. The promise of “one shepherd,” called “my servant David,” appears within this movement toward a covenant of peace; the identity and meaning of that figure remain a matter of interpretation.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 34:1-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_034_33e8b5faed1cb466c9cb760e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}